[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Candidate shape: lifecycles with Jira-style transitions (who may, what must hold, what it says)

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-CANDIDATE-JIRA-TRANSITIONS
TITLE: Candidate shape: lifecycles with Jira-style transitions (who may, what must hold, what it says)
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
PROPOSED SHAPE, one of three, from the 2026-08-29 prior-art spike
(SPIKE-DSL-PRIOR-ART). Not chosen. All three show the SAME worked example so
they compare by eye: a DECISION whose STATUS moves from open to accepted only
when a guard holds, a dirty-edge rule over one relation role, one gate CI can
call, and one actor primitive keyed on an ssh identity.

WHAT IT BORROWS. Jira workflows: one lifecycle per node type, each transition carrying a who-may slot (Jira condition), a data slot (Jira validator) and an error message; ripple and gates are separate records.

THE SHAPE.

  { c, ... }: {                    # c = the DSL's JsonLogic helpers (see condition language)
    canon.lifecycles.DECISION = {
      stateField = "STATUS";  initial = "open";  terminal = [ "accepted" "retired" ];
      transitions.accept = {
        from = "open";  to = "accepted";
        who = [ "human" ];                                  # Jira "condition": actor gate
        when = c.every "Governed_By"                        # Jira "validator": data gate
          (c.inList (c.var "target.DEPTH") [ "interface-settled" "implemented" "verified" ]);
        message = "every governing node must be at least interface-settled";
      };
    };
    canon.relations.Governed_By.ripple = {
      onFields = [ "STATEMENT" ];                           # a change here marks the edge stale
      clearedBy = [ "human" ];                              # who may re-stamp it
    };
    canon.actors = {
      extractor = "ssh-signing-key";                        # an L2 extractor name
      classes.human = [ "SHA256:AbCd..." ];
      default = "agent";
    };
    canon.gates.merge.require = [ "no-stale-edges" "lifecycle-consistent" ];
    # CI runs:  scribe check --gate merge
  }

HOW IT DECOMPOSES ONTO THE HOOK LAYER (MECH-HOOK-MIDDLEWARE-INTERFACE).
transitions.accept -> one PRE hook on scribe.change.set for DECISION.STATUS, python = the generic evaluator, settings = { from; to; who; when; message }.
relations.X.ripple -> POST hook on scribe.change.set of onFields (re-stamps neighbours' stored hash to stale) plus a PRE hook on the re-stamp field checking actor.class in clearedBy.
gates.merge -> the `scribe check --gate merge` command, also wired as a PRE hook on git.push.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
STRENGTHS.
- Reads like the operator's own sentence: who may, what must hold, what it says when it fails.
- Nix checks the lifecycle at eval: from/to values in the grammar enum, every state reachable, initial and terminal present, no two transitions on one (from, to).
- Actor gating is a plain list, so the augment case (add a class) is one line and needs no condition edit.

WEAKNESSES.
- Two ways to say 'who': the who list and an actor clause inside when; pick one or Nix must assert they agree.
- Sites are fixed by construct kind (transition = PRE change, ripple = POST change, gate = check); a rule that wants warn-at-commit and deny-at-check has no home here.
- Automatic transitions have no slot; they become gate conditions reported as due.


Recorded from the spike's synthesis without endorsement; the operator weighs the three by reading the shapes.
